.\" Manual page for pam_ssoossh.
.\" Rendered with: man -l docs/pam_ssoossh.8
.\" Checked with:  mandoc -T lint docs/pam_ssoossh.8
.TH PAM_SSOOSSH 8 "2026-09-05" "pam_ssoossh" "System Manager's Manual"
.SH NAME
pam_ssoossh \- authenticate by having a human approve a certificate request
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B auth sufficient pam_ssoossh.so
.RI "server=" URL
.RI "trusted\-ca\-file=" PATH
.RI [ options ]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pam_ssoossh
authenticates a local account by generating a single\-use SSH keypair,
asking
.BR ssoosshd (8)
to certify it, showing the person at the terminal how to approve the
request, waiting for a human to approve it, and validating the certificate
that comes back against a set of trusted certificate authorities.
.PP
It implements the
.B auth
stack only, through
.B pam_sm_authenticate
and
.BR pam_sm_setcred .
The latter establishes nothing: the certificate is consumed during
authentication and never installed into the session. Do not list the
module on
.BR account ", " password " or " session
lines; it has no code for them.
.PP
It is a reimplementation in C of the Go module of the same name in the
ssoossh repository, and reads the same
.BR pam.d (5)
lines. A stanza that works with the Go module keeps working here, with the
differences named in
.B COMPATIBILITY
below.
.SH MODES
Two flows exist, and
.B mode=auto
picks between them for every login.
.SS The browser flow
On a machine with a browser, or in a session that came from one, the
module prints one message through the PAM conversation and waits:
.PP
.RS
.nf
Approve this request in your browser:
https://ssoossh.example.com/approve/6f1c0a5e\-1f2b\-4c3d\-8e9f\-0a1b2c3d4e5f
.fi
.RE
.PP
Nothing is read back. The login proceeds when someone with the right to
approve it does so in the browser, or fails when they deny it, when
.B timeout
elapses, or when the person at the terminal presses Ctrl\-C.
.SS The console flow
On a machine with no browser in front of it \(em a virtual console, a serial
line, a hypervisor or BMC console \(em an approval URL is a link nobody can
follow. The console flow prints a short code and, where it fits, a QR code
carrying the complete verification URL, and the approver carries either to a
device that does have a browser:
.PP
.RS
.nf
Approve this login from a device with a browser.

  Go to:  https://ssoossh.example.com/console
  Code:   K7M4\-QP2X

  \[u2588]\[u2588]\[u2588]\[u2588]\[u2588]\[u2588]\[u2588]\[u2588]\[u2588]\[u2588]\[u2588]\[u2588]\[u2588]\[u2588]\[u2588]\[u2588]\[u2588]
  \[u2588]\[u2588] \[u2584]\[u2584]\[u2584]\[u2584]\[u2584] \[u2588] \[u2584]\[u2580] \[u2588] \[u2584]\[u2584]\[u2584]\[u2584]\[u2584] \[u2588]\[u2588]
  ...
.fi
.RE
.PP
The code is Crockford base32 in two groups; the QR code is drawn from Unicode
half\-block characters and needs a terminal font that has them. It is
omitted, and the code stands alone, when the message would not fit.
.SS Which flow runs
.B mode=auto
chooses the console flow when
.B PAM_RHOST
is empty \(em nothing came in over the network \(em and
.B PAM_TTY
names a physical terminal: a Linux or FreeBSD virtual console, a serial
line, a hypervisor console, or
.B /dev/console
itself. Everything else gets the browser flow, and each exclusion is
deliberate. A pseudo\-terminal
.RI ( pts/N )
is a terminal emulator or an SSH session, where a browser is a keystroke
away. An unset
.B PAM_TTY
is a cron job or a script, with no human at all. A set
.B PAM_RHOST
means the person is at their own machine, which has a browser, however
console\-like the terminal on this end looks.
.PP
The decision is therefore per login rather than per host: the same
.BR pam.d (5)
line does the right thing for a serial console and for an SSH session.
.SH OPTIONS
Arguments are
.IB key = value
words on the
.BR pam.d (5)
line. A value containing spaces uses libpam's bracket form,
.IR key=[a\ value] .
.TP
.BI server= URL
Required. The
.B ssoosshd
base URL. A missing scheme becomes
.BR https:// ,
and trailing slashes are trimmed. Absent or empty is
.BR PAM_USER_UNKNOWN ,
returned before any key is generated or any socket opened. Longer than
512 bytes is
.BR PAM_NO_MODULE_DATA .
.TP
.BI trusted\-ca\-file= PATH
Required. CA public keys in
.BR authorized_keys (5)
format, one per line, read on every attempt so a deployment can rotate CAs
without a restart. See
.BR pam_ssoossh\-ca (5).
A key of an algorithm this build cannot verify is skipped with a warning
naming it and the line; if none usable remains, that is
.BR PAM_NO_MODULE_DATA ,
the same code an unreadable file produces.
.TP
.BI principals\-map= PATH
Optional in the module, needed in practice. Maps a local account to the
certificate principals allowed to assume it \(em see
.BR pam_ssoossh\-principals (5).
A map that is configured but fails to load logs a warning and falls back
to requiring the certificate to carry the exact account name; it does not
fail the login, so a mistyped path degrades to the stricter default rather
than locking every account out of the host. A map that loads is
authoritative: an account with no entry in it is denied.
.TP
.BI skew\-tolerance= DURATION
Default
.BR 2s .
Applied symmetrically to both ends of the certificate's validity window, to
absorb clock skew between the issuing server and this host. Prefer keeping
clocks synchronised to widening this: a wider window is also a longer life
for a certificate that leaks.
.TP
.BI timeout= DURATION
Default
.BR 60s .
Bounds the whole attempt, not only the waiting half. The server's own
expiry for the request bounds it further when that is the earlier of the
two.
.TP
.BR insecure\-skip\-verify [ =true | =false ]
Default off. Disables TLS verification of the
.B ssoosshd
certificate. With this on, nothing the server says is authenticated,
including the URL that reaches the terminal; the module logs a warning on
every attempt that uses it. A value that is neither
.BR true " nor " false
is treated as
.BR false .
.TP
.BI mode= auto | sudo | console
Default
.BR auto ,
described under
.B MODES
above.
.BR sudo " and " console
force a flow. An unrecognised value is
.BR PAM_NO_MODULE_DATA ,
never a silent fall back. Console mode is compiled in on Linux and FreeBSD
only; on macOS
.B mode=console
is refused with the same code and
.B mode=auto
always uses the browser flow.
.TP
.BR debug [ = VALUE ]
Default off. Logs the decision for each of the four checks, the URL sent
to the terminal, and the request context at
.BR LOG_DEBUG .
The bare word, or any value other than
.BR false ,
turns it on. The legacy value
.B debug=stdout
is accepted and treated as plain
.BR debug :
writing to a stream that belongs to
.BR sudo (8)
is the one thing this module never does.
.PP
Durations use Go's
.B time.ParseDuration
grammar \(em
.BR 2s ", " 500ms ", " 1h30m ", " 1.5h
\(em because that is what existing
.BR pam.d (5)
lines already contain. An unparseable duration is silently the default.
An unknown argument is ignored.
.SH THE FOUR CHECKS
Run in order on the certificate the server issues; every failure produces
.B PAM_AUTH_ERR
and logs its reason at
.BR LOG_ERR .
.IP 1. 4
.B CA signature
\(em the certificate is cryptographically signed by a key in
.BR trusted\-ca\-file .
Not a string comparison against that file's contents.
.IP 2. 4
.B Key binding
\(em the certificate's public key is the one generated for this attempt.
Without it, checks 1, 3 and 4 passing together would accept any CA\-signed
certificate carrying the right principal, including one issued to somebody
else's keypair.
.IP 3. 4
.B Principal
\(em the certificate's principals authorise this local account, through
the principals map when one is loaded, and by exact name otherwise.
.IP 4. 4
.B Validity window
\(em now is within
.RB [ valid_after ", " valid_before ]
plus or minus
.BR skew\-tolerance .
.PP
The per\-attempt keypair is always ECDSA P\-384, which every platform's
crypto supports, so only the CA side is ever constrained by
.B CERTIFICATE ALGORITHMS
below.
.SH RETURN VALUES
.TP
.B PAM_SUCCESS
The certificate passed all four checks.
.TP
.B PAM_USER_UNKNOWN
.B pam_get_user
failed, or
.B server
is not configured.
.TP
.B PAM_NO_MODULE_DATA
The argument vector could not be read, an argument is malformed or too
long, or the trusted CA file is missing or holds no usable key.
.TP
.B PAM_AUTH_ERR
The request resolved and the answer was no: denied, expired, the server
could not issue, the certificate did not parse, or a check failed. Also a
timeout.
.TP
.B PAM_IGNORE
The person pressed Ctrl\-C at the approval prompt. The module contributes
nothing and the stack continues to whatever follows it.
.TP
.B PAM_AUTHINFO_UNAVAIL
.B ssoosshd
could not be reached, or answered with something that was not an answer.
This lets a
.B sufficient
stack fall through, so an ssoossh outage does not become a
.BR sudo (8)
outage on every host. A server that answered and said no is
.B PAM_AUTH_ERR
instead.
.TP
.B PAM_ABORT
The keypair or the HTTP client could not be constructed.
.SH SERVICES
Which
.BR pam.d (5)
services to put the module in is an operator's decision, and the packages
ship a commented example for each of the usual ones under
.I examples/pam.d
(see
.BR FILES ).
The shape is always the same: one
.B auth sufficient
line above the service's existing password authentication.
.TP
.B sudo
The original use. The browser flow, since a
.BR sudo (8)
session has a terminal emulator or SSH session behind it.
.TP
.B su
The same, after the
.B pam_rootok.so
line that lets root switch without authenticating.
.TP
.B sshd
Works through keyboard\-interactive authentication, which is how
.BR sshd (8)
runs PAM and relays the module's message to the client. Needs
.B UsePAM yes
and
.B KbdInteractiveAuthentication yes
in
.BR sshd_config (5).
Always the browser flow, since
.B PAM_RHOST
is set. Public\-key logins are unaffected unless
.B AuthenticationMethods
requires both.
.TP
.BR login ", " getty
A console login, so
.B mode=auto
runs the console flow. Consider gating the line with
.B pam_succeed_if.so
so that root, or accounts outside a group, never see it, and leave
.B pam_securetty.so
where it is.
.PP
Do not add the module to a shared include such as
.IR common\-auth ", " system\-auth " or " base\-auth :
screen lockers, display managers and
.BR polkit (8)
read those too, and an approval prompt that cannot be seen is a lock\-out.
.PP
Test from a second, already\-open root session, never from the one whose
service is being edited, and keep a copy of the file being changed.
.SH PRINCIPALS
A certificate's principals are decided by
.BR ssoosshd ,
and for a PAM request they carry the
.I approver's
identity \(em the username of the person who approved, and any other
accounts the server has recorded them as holding \(em never the local
account name that was asked for. A host whose local accounts are not
spelled exactly as the identity provider spells its users therefore needs a
principals map for anyone to log in at all. See
.BR pam_ssoossh\-principals (5).
.SH CERTIFICATE ALGORITHMS
Which CA key types work is decided by the crypto the platform ships.
Anything unsupported fails with an error naming the algorithm, never with a
vague signature failure.
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l l l.
CA key type@Linux, FreeBSD@macOS
_
ecdsa\-sha2\-nistp256/384/521@yes@yes
rsa\-sha2\-256, rsa\-sha2\-512@yes@yes
ssh\-ed25519@yes@yes (macOS 14+)
ssh\-rsa (SHA\-1)@refused@refused
.TE
.PP
.B ssh\-rsa
names RSA with SHA\-1, which OpenSSH has disabled by default since 8.8. It
is refused by policy on every platform.
.PP
On macOS, Ed25519 is verified through a Security.framework interface Apple
exports but does not document. The module resolves it at runtime, self\-tests
it before use, and names the outcome in the version line it logs on every
authentication:
.B crypto: Security.framework (Ed25519 SPI ok)
when it works, and
.B (no Ed25519 SPI)
or
.B (Ed25519 SPI FAILED self\-test)
when it does not \(em in which case an
.B ssh\-ed25519
CA is skipped with a warning naming its type, and every other CA type still
works.
.SH FIPS MODE
The module makes no cryptographic choices a FIPS policy could object to,
and defers the ones it cannot make to the host. The per\-attempt keypair is
ECDSA P\-384 on every platform. Which CA algorithms verify is whatever the
host's crypto library allows in its current configuration, found out by
asking it rather than by table:
.IP \(bu 4
RSA and ECDSA certificates go through the same OpenSSL calls with FIPS on
or off. Where the FIPS module refuses one \(em an RSA CA below the approved
key size, say \(em the check fails and the log line carries OpenSSL's own
reason, not a claim that the certificate was malformed.
.IP \(bu 4
Ed25519 is the algorithm a FIPS configuration commonly lacks. On first use
the module has the host's OpenSSL verify a known RFC 8032 vector; if that
is refused, an
.B ssh\-ed25519
CA is skipped with a warning naming FIPS, every other CA type keeps
working, and a certificate signed by such a CA is refused as unsupported
rather than as a bad signature. RHEL 8's FIPS module has no EdDSA; RHEL 9's
OpenSSL 3.5 does, and there Ed25519 works in FIPS mode.
.IP \(bu 4
TLS to the server is
.BR libcurl (3)
over the same OpenSSL, under the same policy.
.PP
FIPS mode is detected from the kernel's
.I /proc/sys/crypto/fips_enabled
where there is one, and from the library's own flag \(em the default
property query on OpenSSL 3, the mode switch on 1.1.1 \(em so a host that
forces it through OpenSSL configuration alone is also reported. The state
appears in the version line of every authentication as
.BR "fips: on" " or " "fips: off" .
On macOS there is no such switch \(em Apple's corecrypto is validated as
shipped \(em and the line carries no field.
.SH PAM ITEMS
The module reads
.BR PAM_USER ,
which names the account, and
.BR PAM_SERVICE ", " PAM_TTY ", " PAM_RHOST " and " PAM_RUSER ,
which are sent to
.B ssoosshd
as part of the request's context.
.BR PAM_TTY " and " PAM_RHOST
also decide the flow, as described under
.BR MODES .
.SH REQUEST CONTEXT
Every request, in either flow, carries what this process can say about
itself and where it is, so the approver can tell a request they caused from
one they did not: the hostname; the PAM service, terminal, remote host and
requesting user from the items above; the host process's command line
(Linux and FreeBSD); the calling uid, pid and parent pid; the machine
identifier
.RI ( /etc/machine\-id
on Linux, the host UUID elsewhere); the operating system, from
.I /etc/os\-release
and
.BR uname (1);
this module's name and version; the configured
.BR mode ;
the host's clock; and the SHA256 fingerprint of each key in
.BR trusted\-ca\-file ,
up to eight.
.PP
Every one of these is self\-reported by an unauthenticated caller. The
module verifies none of them before sending, and the server shows them as
claims, not facts; a field the host cannot supply is left out rather than
guessed.
Everything written to the terminal goes through the PAM conversation
function the application supplied; the module never opens the tty itself.
.SH NETWORK
Requests go over HTTPS with TLS 1.3 only, verified against the operating
system's trust store; there is no option for a private CA bundle other than
installing the CA into that store. Redirects are not followed. Connecting
is bounded at ten seconds and the whole attempt by
.BR timeout .
While waiting, the module holds one streaming connection open for the
approval and reconnects if it drops.
.PP
The HTTP client is
.BR libcurl (3),
which honours the
.BR http_proxy ", " https_proxy " and " no_proxy
environment variables. Inside
.BR sudo (8)
those are normally scrubbed from the environment, so a host that needs a
proxy for the module has to arrange it in the calling process.
.SH LOGGING
Everything goes to
.BR syslog (3)
under
.BR LOG_AUTHPRIV .
Nothing is written to stdout or stderr.
.BR openlog (3)
and
.BR closelog (3)
are never called: the first mutates process\-global state and the second
closes a descriptor the host process may be holding, and inside
.BR sudo (8)
neither is this module's to touch. Every message carries its own
.B pam_ssoossh:
prefix instead.
.PP
Each attempt logs one unconditional line naming the module version and the
crypto and HTTP libraries actually linked into the process:
.PP
.RS
.nf
pam_ssoossh: 1.0.0 | crypto: OpenSSL 1.1.1k | fips: off | http: libcurl/7.61.1 OpenSSL/1.1.1k
.fi
.RE
.PP
The module links crypto rather than shipping it, so which OpenSSL is
resident in
.BR sudo (8),
and whether it is in FIPS mode, is a property of the host. That line is
what makes the question answerable across a fleet by grepping syslog.
Failures log their reason at
.BR LOG_ERR ,
a successful authentication at
.BR LOG_INFO ,
and the per\-check reasoning at
.B LOG_DEBUG
when
.B debug
is set.
.PP
Text that reaches the terminal is filtered first: the approval URL is
reduced to the RFC 3986 character set, the console code to its base32
alphabet, and the QR code to the three block characters it is drawn from.
A server response that lost characters to that filter is logged as a
warning, because it is either an
.B ssoosshd
that changed shape or a hostile one.
.SH COMPATIBILITY
Differences from the Go module worth knowing when swapping one for the
other:
.IP \(bu 4
The console flow and
.B mode=
exist only here.
.IP \(bu 4
.B PAM_IGNORE
is returned on Ctrl\-C here; the Go module returns
.B PAM_AUTH_ERR
for that case.
.IP \(bu 4
.B ssh\-rsa
(SHA\-1) CAs are refused here and accepted there.
.IP \(bu 4
Log lines are prefixed
.B pam_ssoossh:
here; the Go module logs under the syslog tag
.BR ssoossh .
.IP \(bu 4
Text sent to the terminal is filtered here, as described under
.BR LOGGING .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/pam.d/*
The service stanzas that load this module.
.TP
.I /lib/*/security/pam_ssoossh.so
The module, wherever
.BR libpam (8)
looks on this platform:
.I /usr/lib/<triplet>/security
on Debian and Ubuntu,
.I /usr/lib64/security
on RHEL and its rebuilds,
.I /lib/security
on Alpine.
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/pam\-ssoossh/examples/
Shipped by the packages: a commented
.BR pam.d (5)
fragment for each service under
.IR pam.d/ ,
and a
.I principals.yaml
to start from.
.TP
.I /etc/ssoossh/ca.pub
.TQ
.I /etc/ssoossh/principals.yaml
The paths the examples use. Nothing in the module fixes them.
.SH EXAMPLES
The documented stanza for
.BR sudo :
.PP
.RS
.nf
auth    sufficient  pam_ssoossh.so server=https://ssoossh.example.com \\
                    trusted\-ca\-file=/etc/ssoossh/ca.pub \\
                    principals\-map=/etc/ssoossh/principals.yaml \\
                    skew\-tolerance=2s timeout=60s
auth    required    pam_unix.so
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B sufficient
means an approval ends the auth stack and anything else \(em a cancellation,
a denial, a timeout, an unreachable server \(em hands over to
.BR pam_unix (8).
A denied approval therefore reaches the password prompt. That is the
intended posture rather than an oversight: ssoossh is an additional way to
authenticate, not a gate that can lock an operator out of a host when the
browser flow is unavailable.
.PP
The same line forcing the console flow on a
.BR login (1)
service, guarded so root never sees it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
auth    [success=ignore default=1]  pam_succeed_if.so user != root quiet
auth    sufficient  pam_ssoossh.so server=https://ssoossh.example.com \\
                    trusted\-ca\-file=/etc/ssoossh/ca.pub \\
                    principals\-map=/etc/ssoossh/principals.yaml \\
                    mode=console timeout=120s
.fi
.RE
